{
  "title": "Una casetta inutile, gentile signora",
  "translation": "A useless little house, dear madam",
  "topics": "Prefixes and suffixes, register and polite language",
  "grammar": {
    "explain": [
      {
        "h": "Changing the meaning with an ending"
      },
      "Italian can add a shade of size or feeling to a word with a suffix, so a single word does the work of an adjective.",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Suffix",
            "Meaning",
            "Examples"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "-ino, -etto, -ello",
              "small, dear",
              "tavol**ino**, cas**etta**, albero → alber**ello**"
            ],
            [
              "-one",
              "big",
              "libr**one**, nas**one**"
            ],
            [
              "-accio",
              "bad, ugly",
              "temp**accio**, parol**accia**"
            ],
            [
              "-uccio",
              "small and endearing",
              "cas**uccia**, cald**uccio**"
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      "A feminine noun can become masculine with *-one*: *la porta → il portone*. Be careful with words that only look altered: *il bottone* isn't a big *botte*, *il lampone* is a raspberry, and *il mattone* is a brick.",
      {
        "h": "Changing the meaning with a prefix"
      },
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Prefix",
            "Meaning",
            "Examples"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "in- (im-, il-, ir-)",
              "not",
              "**in**utile, **im**possibile, **il**legale, **ir**regolare"
            ],
            [
              "s-",
              "opposite, not",
              "**s**contento, **s**fortuna"
            ],
            [
              "dis-",
              "opposite",
              "**dis**ordine, **dis**onesto"
            ],
            [
              "ri-",
              "again",
              "**ri**fare, **ri**leggere"
            ],
            [
              "pre-",
              "before",
              "**pre**vedere"
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      "*In-* changes with the next letter, so that it is easier to say: *im-* before *p, b, m*, *il-* before *l* and *ir-* before *r*.",
      {
        "h": "Making new words from old ones"
      },
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "From",
            "Ending",
            "Result"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Adjective → adverb",
              "-mente",
              "lento → lenta**mente**"
            ],
            [
              "Verb → noun",
              "-zione",
              "spiegare → spiega**zione**"
            ],
            [
              "Verb → person",
              "-tore / -trice",
              "scrivere → scrit**tore**, scrit**trice**"
            ],
            [
              "Adjective → noun",
              "-ità",
              "facile → facil**ità**"
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "h": "Register: choosing how formal to be"
      },
      "Register is the level of formality that suits the situation. Formal Italian uses *Lei* with the verb in the third person, the conditional for requests, and more precise words.",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Informal",
            "Formal"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Ciao!",
              "Buongiorno. / Salve."
            ],
            [
              "{ip:Puoi} aiutarmi?",
              "{cop:Potrebbe} aiutarmi, per cortesia?"
            ],
            [
              "Ti {ip:scrivo} per…",
              "Le {ip:scrivo} per…"
            ],
            [
              "Ti {ip:chiedo} scusa.",
              "La prego di scusarmi."
            ],
            [
              "dare",
              "fornire"
            ],
            [
              "dire",
              "comunicare"
            ],
            [
              "avere",
              "disporre di"
            ],
            [
              "chiedere",
              "richiedere"
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      "In a formal letter, open with *Gentile Signora* or *Egregio Dottore*, and close with *Cordiali saluti* or *Distinti saluti*. *Ciao* and *un abbraccio* are for friends."
    ]
  },
